WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.389 --> 00:00:01.222
(simple rhythmic music)

00:00:01.222 --> 00:00:03.725
- This is Yuni, a two year old binturong.

00:00:03.725 --> 00:00:07.735
(simple rhythmic music)

00:00:07.735 --> 00:00:10.377
With a face like a cat,
a body like a bear,

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:13.517
and a tail like a monkey,
these reclusive creatures

00:00:13.517 --> 00:00:15.267
are unlike any other.

00:00:16.218 --> 00:00:18.569
Binturongs are native to
the tropical rainforests

00:00:18.569 --> 00:00:21.441
of Southeast Asia, and
spend most of their time

00:00:21.441 --> 00:00:23.191
high in the treetops.

00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:25.984
They are excellent climbers.

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:28.668
Their long prehensile
tails act as another limb

00:00:28.668 --> 00:00:31.082
as they move through the trees.

00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:33.336
Even when asleep, their tails are anchored

00:00:33.336 --> 00:00:36.182
securely around a branch.

00:00:36.182 --> 00:00:38.804
The binturongs flexibility
doesn't end there,

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:43.261
their ankles can rotate 180
degrees to maintain a grip,

00:00:43.261 --> 00:00:46.755
even when climbing down a tree head first.

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:49.535
Perhaps the most curious
trait of the binturong

00:00:49.535 --> 00:00:52.069
is its smell, buttered popcorn.

00:00:52.069 --> 00:00:54.975
While pleasing to a human
nose, the binturong’s scent

00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:58.011
is a way to mark its
territory and to warn rivals

00:00:58.011 --> 00:00:59.946
to move along.

00:00:59.946 --> 00:01:01.981
The binturong faces many threats,

00:01:01.981 --> 00:01:04.668
the worst being habitat destruction.

00:01:04.668 --> 00:01:06.121
Over the past two decades

00:01:06.121 --> 00:01:09.703
their population has fallen precipitously.

00:01:09.703 --> 00:01:11.273
This is the binturong.

00:01:11.273 --> 00:01:14.273
(soft simple music)

